  "diagnoses": {
    "T1": "A histogram update has three steps: read the count, add one, and write the result. If two threads read the same count before either writes, one update can be lost. In ten runs with eight threads, the unsafe version lost about three million counts per run. Atomic integer updates would protect the counts, but floating-point sums could still differ because the order of addition affects rounding. The safe version gives each thread its own bins and stores the weights as integer units. It merges the integer totals before converting back to floating point, and the results match bit for bit at 1, 2, 4, and 8 threads.",
    "T2": "In the unpadded array, different threads can write to different values on the same cache line. Under MESI-style cache coherence, a core needs write ownership of that line, so a write can invalidate copies held by other cores. Padding places the active slots 320 bytes apart, and both versions still write to memory inside the loop. Both versions produced the same values at every thread count. With eight threads, the unpadded version took 56.44 ms and the padded version took 55.24 ms, a speedup of about 1.02x in this run.",
    "T3": "Static scheduling gives each thread a fixed range of rows, but some rows require more operations than others. The dynamic scheduler mixes light and heavy rows, and threads use an atomic counter to claim batches of 16 rows. Each thread stops claiming work when it reaches its operation target or the queue is empty. With eight threads, both methods returned exactly the same scores. The dynamic imbalance ratio was 1.014, below the required 1.15, while the static ratio was slightly lower at 1.005. The dynamic kernel itself took 6.99 ms versus 7.76 ms for the static kernel."
  },
